#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6617" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y''+y=\\tan{x}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n$\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x, \\qquad u_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\tan x\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x, \\qquad y_2(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}(x)y_{2}^{′}(x) - y_{1}^{′}(x)y_{2}(x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x)^{′} - (\\cos x)^{′}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\cos x) - (-\\sin x)(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x \\\\\n&= 1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫(f(x)y_{2}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\tan x\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= \\sin x - \\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫(f(x)y_{1}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\tan x\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= ∫\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\tan x + \\sec x &) + (-\\cos x)(\\sin x) \\\\ =-\\cos x\\ln & \\tan x + \\sec x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= A\\cos x + B\\sin x- \\cos x\\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid _(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-6618" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx, \\qquad u_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(xe^{x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(e^{x} + xe^{x}) - (e^{x})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= e^{2x} + xe^{2x} - xe^{2x} \\\\\n&= e^{2x} \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot xe^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&=-18∫x^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-6x^{3} \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot e^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&= 18∫xdx \\\\\n&= 9x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (-6x^{3})e^{x} + (9x^{2})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&=-6x^{3}e^{x} + 9x^{3}e^{x} \\\\\n&= 3x^{3}e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x} + 3x^{3}e^{x}_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb69a321-2b4b-59e4-91a1-294654418129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + 2\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228387} {:answer/id #uuid "ce59769f-d45f-52f4-a21f-948a616ccbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(2x\\arctan x - \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228388} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ed170-d1a3-5a8f-802c-adf79dbf8451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + 4\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228389} {:answer/id #uuid "1922057e-9c1b-593f-84d6-0c5c14e6eca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x\\arctan x + 4\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228390} {:answer/id #uuid "7d648f51-f6f2-5f4f-849c-040d10ff79ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce59769f-d45f-52f4-a21f-948a616ccbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(2x\\arctan x - \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228388} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9261647-99e3-5267-a388-8b64afbe051c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228386}] :question/id #uuid "12871450-b62c-5f51-815b-c8231596a048" :question/math-academy-id "q-337391" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y = \\frac{2e^{3x}}{1 + x^{2}}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a14792f2-5973-59c5-9c53-4108312545d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\sin 2x + \\cos 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228409} {:answer/id #uuid "ac229b5c-ac37-58e8-a2a7-8f3098c4869c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228410} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a6fd65-79da-5c8f-822d-7346d7fb32cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228411} {:answer/id #uuid "e44f7165-d39a-58b9-ae7f-9d82e8ba2e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228412} {:answer/id #uuid "fc169e50-ea5d-5ece-8615-5fd5867208cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc169e50-ea5d-5ece-8615-5fd5867208cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228413} :answer-field/id #uuid "874bf077-7f9c-52f1-8b97-fdb53377c843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228408}] :question/id #uuid "00fa4d0d-a8d6-536d-9f44-d4c9796c3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337950" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} + 4y = 2\\tan x$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n$∫\\sin 2x\\tan xdx|= x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x; ∫\\cos 2x\\tan xdx|= \\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x - \\frac{1}{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a524bc-dbe0-527a-9025-3d60ee66662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369114} {:answer/id #uuid "4f701a29-414c-5242-af93-500e7082a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369115} {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbfd963-2906-5722-b152-e3c6726d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369117} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3d0790-793b-5370-982b-173ea365929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} :answer-field/id #uuid "210210bc-cd82-5454-b1dc-84e092e994aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d64847-1769-5602-97fa-762ad6861828" :question/math-academy-id "q-338142" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d95791-2cc7-5a8b-962f-73c236a5c448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369121} {:answer/id #uuid "de4cbfee-dc26-5f53-9de8-a54d1fed0a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc64cc80-c370-594d-8ddb-00cdb5c8bcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369123} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} {:answer/id #uuid "c11e43fe-ba7a-53c0-90d7-a59aa3b96b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fdd2a3-162b-5f4d-bd71-bfbd4cb30ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb7b8734-1edc-5c36-87f9-ef5befb2dd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-338767" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}]}
